# C4 — Reversibility characterizes self-adjointness of a Markov kernel

Building on `Bridges.DiscreteHodgeRandomWalk`, this file proves the equivalence
that upgrades reversibility from a property of graph-derived walks to a full
characterization: a kernel `P` on `Fin n` is reversible with respect to a measure
`π` **iff** it is self-adjoint for the `π`-weighted inner product
`⟨f, g⟩_π = Σᵢ πᵢ fᵢ gᵢ`, **iff** the associated weight kernel `wᵢⱼ = πᵢ Pᵢⱼ`
is symmetric.  The last clause says reversible Markov chains are *exactly*
weighted graphs, canonically.

## Main results

* `reversible_iff_selfAdjoint`           — detailed balance ⟺ `π`-self-adjointness.
* `weightKernel_isSymm_iff_reversible`   — symmetric weight kernel ⟺ detailed balance.
* `reversible_tfae`                       — the three conditions are equivalent.
-/

import Mathlib
import Bridges.DiscreteHodgeRandomWalk

noncomputable section

open Matrix BigOperators Finset

namespace DiscreteHodge

variable {n : ℕ}

/-- `P` is **reversible** with respect to the measure `π` (detailed balance):
    `πᵢ Pᵢⱼ = πⱼ Pⱼᵢ`. -/
def Reversible (π : Fin n → ℝ) (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ i j, π i * P i j = π j * P j i

/-- `P` is **self-adjoint** for the `π`-weighted inner product
    `⟨f, g⟩_π = Σᵢ πᵢ fᵢ gᵢ`: `⟨f, P g⟩_π = ⟨P f, g⟩_π` for all `f, g`. -/
def SelfAdjointPi (π : Fin n → ℝ) (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ f g : Fin n → ℝ,
    ∑ i, π i * f i * (P.mulVec g) i = ∑ i, π i * (P.mulVec f) i * g i

/-
**C4 (core).** A kernel is reversible w.r.t. `π` iff it is self-adjoint for the
    `π`-weighted inner product.
-/
theorem reversible_iff_selfAdjoint (π : Fin n → ℝ) (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) :
    Reversible π P ↔ SelfAdjointPi π P := by
      constructor
      · intro h f g
        simp only [Matrix.mulVec, dotProduct, Finset.mul_sum, Finset.sum_mul]
        rw [Finset.sum_comm]
        refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => Finset.sum_congr rfl fun j _ => ?_
        linear_combination (f j * g i) * h j i
      · intro h i j; specialize h ( fun k => if k = i then 1 else 0 ) ( fun k => if k = j then 1 else 0 ) ; simp_all +decide [ Matrix.mulVec, dotProduct ] ;

/-
**C4 (weight-kernel form).** The weight kernel `wᵢⱼ = πᵢ Pᵢⱼ` is symmetric iff
    `P` is reversible w.r.t. `π`.  Thus reversible chains are exactly weighted graphs.
-/
theorem weightKernel_isSymm_iff_reversible (π : Fin n → ℝ)
    (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) :
    (Matrix.of (fun i j => π i * P i j)).IsSymm ↔ Reversible π P := by
      constructor <;> intro h <;> simp_all +decide [ Matrix.IsSymm, Reversible ];
      · exact fun i j => by simpa using congr_fun ( congr_fun h j ) i;
      · ext i j; aesop;

/-
The three characterizations of reversibility are equivalent.
-/
theorem reversible_tfae (π : Fin n → ℝ) (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) :
    [Reversible π P,
     SelfAdjointPi π P,
     (Matrix.of (fun i j => π i * P i j)).IsSymm].TFAE := by
      tfae_have 1 ↔ 2 := reversible_iff_selfAdjoint π P
      tfae_have 1 ↔ 3 := (weightKernel_isSymm_iff_reversible π P).symm
      tfae_finish

end DiscreteHodge

/-
-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--

## Hypothesis (Hypothesizer)
C4 of the cycle: reversibility of a Markov kernel is not merely *sufficient* for
self-adjointness in the `π`-inner product — it is *equivalent*, and equivalent in
turn to symmetry of the weight kernel `wᵢⱼ = πᵢ Pᵢⱼ`.  The bold reading: reversible
Markov chains and weighted graphs are the *same objects* under a canonical bijection.

## Experiment (Experimenter)
We stated the `π`-inner product self-adjointness elementwise (avoiding any
`InnerProductSpace` instance) and proved the three-way equivalence.  Forward
(reversible ⇒ self-adjoint) is a double-sum manipulation: expand `P.mulVec`, push
products in, apply detailed balance termwise, then `Finset.sum_comm`.  Backward
(self-adjoint ⇒ reversible) probes the bilinear identity with indicator vectors
`Pi.single i 1`, collapsing both sums to a single term `πᵢ Pᵢⱼ = πⱼ Pⱼᵢ`.  The
weight-kernel clause is `Matrix.IsSymm` unfolded, joined by `tfae_finish`.

## Analysis (Analyst)
No positivity of `π` is needed for the equivalence itself — it is a pure bilinear
algebra fact — so we deliberately omitted that hypothesis to obtain the strongest
statement.  Positivity only matters when one wants `⟨·,·⟩_π` to be a genuine inner
product; the characterization survives without it.

## Critique (Critic) — adversarial counterexample search
* The indicator-vector probe is what makes backward non-trivial: a weaker test
  (only `f = g = 1`) would *not* recover detailed balance, since stochasticity plus
  `π` stationarity already give the `1`-test for free.  Distinct `i ≠ j` indicators
  are essential, confirming the result is not vacuous.
* Asymmetric kernels: take `π = 1`, `P = ![![0,1],![0,1]]` (not symmetric); then
  `π₀P₀₁ = 1 ≠ 0 = π₁P₁₀`, so it is not reversible, and indeed self-adjointness
  fails for `f = e₀, g = e₁`.  The equivalence has genuine content.

## Synthesis (PI)
`reversible_tfae` upgrades `walk_reversible` from a property of graph-derived walks
to a structural identification: every reversible chain *is* a weighted graph.  This
is the canonical dictionary entry that the spectral conjectures (C2, C5) build on.
-/